<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mencius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 372–289 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Mengzi, Meng Ke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mencius (Mengzi) was a Confucian philosopher of the 4th century BCE, born in the State of Zou in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. He travelled among the courts of Qi, Wei and Teng urging rulers to govern through benevolence, and taught that human nature is innately good. His conversations, collected in the book Mencius, became one of the Four Books of Confucian education.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mencius.jpg" alt="Mencius" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Half_Portraits_of_the_Great_Sage_and_Virtuous_Men_of_Old_-_Meng_Ke_(%E5%AD%9F%E8%BB%BB).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the fourth century BCE the Tian rulers supported scholars at the Jixia Academy near the city's Ji Gate with stipends and houses; <b>Mencius</b>, Xunzi, Zou Yan, Shen Dao and Tian Pian were among those associated with it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Below the aristocracy lived farmers who worked land on behalf of lineage heads; the later account by <b>Mencius</b> of the well-field system, with eight families farming a shared ninth plot, is probably an idealisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Two great thinkers developed it: <b>Mencius</b>, in the 4th century BCE, who taught that human nature is good and that a ruler who oppresses his people loses the mandate of Heaven and may rightly be overthrown; and Xunzi, in the 3rd century BCE, who held that human nature…</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Classical Chinese is that of the great texts of the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, from the 6th to the 3rd centuries BCE: the Analects of Confucius, the <b>Mencius</b>, the Zhuangzi, the Xunzi, the Han Feizi and the Zuo Commentary on the Spring and Autumn Annals.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Of the sayings of Confucius quoted by his second-generation student <b>Mencius</b>, 75% are not found in the received text of the Analects.</blockquote><a href="/work/analects">Analects · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 4th century BCE Duke Wen of Teng consulted <b>Mencius</b> on mourning rites and the well-field system of land allotment; their conversations form a chapter of the Mencius.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-teng">State of Teng</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zhu. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The great man is he who does not lose his child's-heart.&#8221;</p><footer>Mencius, book 4, part 2 &#183; tr. James Legge, 1861 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_2/The_Works_of_Mencius/chapter08" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The people are the most important element in a nation; the spirits of the land and grain are the next; the sovereign is the lightest.&#8221;</p><footer>Mencius, book 7, part 2 &#183; tr. James Legge, 1861 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_2/The_Works_of_Mencius/chapter14" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;All men have a mind which cannot bear to see the sufferings of others.&#8221;</p><footer>Mencius, book 2, part 1 &#183; tr. James Legge, 1861 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_2/The_Works_of_Mencius/chapter03" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;So, I like life, and I also like righteousness. If I cannot keep the two together, I will let life go, and choose righteousness.&#8221;</p><footer>Mencius, book 6, part 1 &#183; tr. James Legge, 1861 &#183; after saying that, given fish or bear's paws, he would take the bear's paws &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_2/The_Works_of_Mencius/chapter11" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…when Heaven is about to confer a great office on any man, it first exercises his mind with suffering, and his sinews and bones with toil. It exposes his body to hunger, and subjects him to extreme poverty.&#8221;</p><footer>Mencius, book 6, part 2 &#183; tr. James Legge, 1861 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_2/The_Works_of_Mencius/chapter12" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The tendency of man's nature to good is like the tendency of water to flow downwards.&#8221;</p><footer>Mencius, book 6, part 1 &#183; tr. James Legge, 1861 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_2/The_Works_of_Mencius/chapter11" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
